Q: how to call Services namespace? A: <p>Never mind there's no such thing as calling a namespace.</p>
<p>What you posted is a common <em>very bad practice</em> - a low level Data Access Object over a high level ORM. EF is an high level ORM dealing with entities, not a database connection or driver dealing with tables. It doesn't need low-level CRUD Data Access Objects.</p>
<p>A DbContext is a Unit-of-Work that loads an <em>entire</em> graph of related objects <em>at once</em>, detects changes to <em>all</em> the objects it tracks and persists <em>all</em> of them in a single transaction when <code>SaveChanges</code> is called. It doesn't even keep any connections open, it only open connections briefly to load the original objects and persist all changes and closes them immediatelly</p>
<p>That <code>Add</code> can easily end up deleting 42 objects Also, it breaks transactions, optimistic concurrency and the disconnected operation mode.</p>
<p>The &quot;Service&quot; isn't a service at all, it contains the methods of the typical &quot;repository&quot; bug. Changing the name doesn't change the fact it's bad.</p>
<p>Even your own <code>Program</code> uses  :</p>
<pre><code>context.Suppliers.Add(supplier);
context.Products.Add(product);
context.SaveChanges();
</code></pre>
<p>On top of that, the in-memory provider is <em>not</em> a database at all. It's only meant for quick smoke tests.  Essentially, it's just a Dictionary holding the objects you provided. When you query, you get back the original object without any changes.</p>
<p>Had you written <code>product.Supplier=supplier</code> (the normal way of working with entities), you'd be able to use <code>p.Supplier?.Name</code>. Right now though, nothing's stored there.</p>
<p>Instead of the in-memory provider use SQLite in in-memory mode. This is shown in <a href="https://www.meziantou.net/testing-ef-core-in-memory-using-sqlite.htm" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Testing EF Core in Memory using SQLite</a>.</p>
<p>All you really need to do is configure the provider :</p>
<pre><code>var options = new DbContextOptionsBuilder&lt;SampleDbContext&gt;()
    .UseSqlite(&quot;DataSource=:memory:&quot;)
    .Options;
</code></pre>
<p>And call <code>context.Database.EnsureCreatedAsync();</code> each time you use a new context. If all your test fits in that UoW/context, there's nothing else to do</p>
<pre><code>    var options = new DbContextOptionsBuilder&lt;SampleDbContext&gt;()
        .UseSqlite(&quot;DataSource=:memory:&quot;)
        .Options;

    // Create the dabase schema
    using (var context = new SampleDbContext(options))
    {
        await context.Database.EnsureCreatedAsync();
    } // The connection is closed, so the database is destroyed
</code></pre>
<p>Keep in mind that, as the article explains, when the DbContext (ie the overall transaction scope) gets disposed the <em>database</em> itself gets disposed. To use the same database in multiple transactions/contexts, you need to control the connection manually :</p>
<pre><code>    using (var connection = new SqliteConnection(&quot;DataSource=:memory:&quot;))
    {
        connection.Open();

        var options = new DbContextOptionsBuilder&lt;SampleDbContext&gt;()
            .UseSqlite(connection) // Set the connection explicitly, so it won't be closed automatically by EF
            .Options;

        // Create the dabase schema
        // You can use MigrateAsync if you use Migrations
        using (var context = new SampleDbContext(options))
        {
            await context.Database.EnsureCreatedAsync();
        } // The connection is not closed, so the database still exists

        using (var context = new SampleDbContext(options))
        {
            var user = new User() { Email = &quot;test@sample.com&quot; };
            context.Users.Add(user);
            await context.SaveChangesAsync();
        }

        using (var context = new SampleDbContext(options))
        {
            var count = await context.Users.CountAsync();
            Assert.AreEqual(1, count);

            var u = await context.Users.FirstOrDefaultAsync(user =&gt; user.Email == &quot;test@sample.com&quot;);
            Assert.IsNotNull(u);
        }
    }
</code></pre>
<p><strong>PS</strong>  That <code>GetAll()</code> is a big bug - it loads the entire table and <em>all</em> related suppliers in memory. No application ever wants that. Such a call makes sense if you want to load lookup data but then, you'd use something like <code>ToLookup</code> or <code>ToDictionary</code> to generate the cache,  eg</p>
<pre><code>_productTypes=context.ProductTypes.ToLookup(t=&gt;t.Code);
...
void SomeOtherMethod(...);
{
    var productType=_productTypes[&quot;blah&quot;];
    ...
}
</code></pre>
